<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">North China Plain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> Plain is a large, fertile lowland in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> formed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. It has been a core agricultural region of Chinese states. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river-floods-of-1344">Yellow River floods of 1344</a> devastated its agriculture and disrupted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> grain supply, contributing to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-china-plain.jpg" alt="North China Plain" width="320" height="273" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanguole · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Northern_Plain_relief_location_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The North China Plain is a downfaulted rift basin formed in the late Paleogene and Neogene and later modified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> deposits. It is the largest alluvial plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Yanshan Mountains bound it on the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihang-mountains">Taihang Mountains</a> on the west and the Dabie Mountains on the south, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> and the Bohai Sea lie to the east. It covers about 409,500 square kilometres, most of it less than 50 metres above sea level, and takes in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. The part around the middle and lower Yellow River, the Central Plain, formed the original centre of Chinese civilisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The plain runs largely uninterrupted by mountains, so communication by horse is rapid and its spoken language is relatively uniform; historians connect this with the tendency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s political centre to lie here. Imperial capitals stood on it, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, at various points the capital of the Han, Jin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang dynasties, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a>. Confucius lived and taught in the State of Lu, on the plain, from 551 to 479 BCE.</p><p>The fertile soil merges with the steppes and deserts to the north, so the plain was open to nomadic invaders, which prompted the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>. Its weather is unpredictable, and the flat land floods massively when river works are damaged. Many historians propose that these conditions encouraged a centralised state able to manage granaries, hydraulic works and fortifications. In the winter of 1957 to 1958 the first project of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> was the speeding up of waterworks here.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> lies on the north-eastern edge of the plain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> near its coast, and Jinan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. It is one of the most densely populated regions in the world and a major farming region, producing wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and vegetables. Because of its yellow soil it is nicknamed the Land of the yellow earth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North China Plain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jurchen were a minority in an empire whose census of 1207 counted more than 45 million people, most of them Han Chinese farmers of the <b>North China Plain</b>, alongside Khitan, Bohai and other peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin army crossed the <b>North China Plain</b> to Kaifeng, and in the Jingkang Incident it captured the capital, the retired Emperor Huizong, his successor Emperor Qinzong and most of the Imperial court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Yellow River floods of 1344 the river burst its dykes and shifted its course, devastating agriculture across the <b>North China Plain</b>, creating great numbers of refugees and disrupting the Grand Canal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On the <b>North China Plain</b> villages of many surnames worship tutelary gods of nature in “communities of the gods”, with large temple gatherings and a pantheon of more ancient gods; folk sects and mother goddesses are strongest in the central plains and the northeast, where Han religion met…</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rise followed the decline of the Longshan culture on the <b>North China Plain</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory opened the <b>North China Plain</b> to Tuoba Gui.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-8">8 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
